  "title": "The SSH default overrides trusted GIT_SSH",
  "file": "scripts/push-git.sh",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An operator uses an environment setting to select an SSH program that supplies their working authentication. Factory pushes bypass that program and fail when the default SSH program cannot authenticate.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "In a temporary committed repository, set GIT_SSH to an executable that records 'trusted' and runs the requested receive-pack locally; set GIT_SSH_VARIANT=ssh. Prepend a directory containing an `ssh` executable that records 'fallback' and exits 1. With no configured core.sshCommand, compare `git -c core.hooksPath=/dev/null push \"$destination\" \"$sha:refs/heads/baseline\"` against the same command through `bash \"$checkout/scripts/push-git.sh\" git`. The destination uses SSH syntax, but both executables operate locally without network access.",
    "output": "Ordinary push: exit 0, baseline branch received. Wrapped push: exit 128, 'fatal: Could not read from remote repository.' Invocation markers, in order: 'trusted', 'fallback'. The bare remote contains only the baseline branch."
   },
   "evidence": "HEAD initializes `ssh_command=ssh`, restores that default when the configuration lookup finds no value, and always injects `-c \"core.sshCommand=$ssh_command\"`. This overrides Git's GIT_SSH selection, as demonstrated by the executable markers. At base, worktreeGit passes the hardened push command directly without injecting core.sshCommand. This differs from G2's lost remote-dependent configuration and G4's forced HTTPS transport."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An owner who sets up SSH for git through the GIT_SSH environment variable (not through config) finds that factory pushes now ignore it and run plain 'ssh'. Pushes that worked before now fail with 'Could not read from remote repository'.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "export GIT_SSH=$TMPDIR/c2/fakessh; git push -q localhost:$TMPDIR/c2/dst.git ${sha}:refs/heads/before; bash scripts/push-git.sh git push -q localhost:$TMPDIR/c2/dst.git ${sha}:refs/heads/after; git -C dst.git branch",
    "output": "plain push rc=0; wrapper push rc=128 'Please make sure you have the correct access rights'; dst.git has only 'before'; the fake GIT_SSH marker was written only by the plain push (its variant probe plus the push)"
   },
   "evidence": "push-git.sh:22-29: `ssh_command=ssh` ... `-c \"core.sshCommand=$ssh_command\"` always sets core.sshCommand. Git gives core.sshCommand precedence over the GIT_SSH environment variable, so a trusted GIT_SSH set in the environment is overridden by the 'ssh' fallback. The request says 'Credentials must keep working exactly as today (the owner's pushes use their existing global helper or SSH setup)'. This is a different mechanism from G2 (remote-dependent config) and G4 (forcing HTTPS)."
  }
 }
